  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\n- Showers and isolated thunderstorms expected this afternoon into \n  Wednesday \n\nRadar shows a line of showers ahead of a cold front approaching the \nnorthwest CWA at present, which should slowly expand south and east \ninto the evening and tonight. HREF mean MUCAPE shows just a few \nhundred joules suggesting little severe threat though scattered \nthunderstorms are possible. High PWATs and 30+ knots of shear may be \nsufficient to support gusts over 30 mph with water loaded \ndowndrafts and heavy rainfall. The front will clear the area \nWednesday afternoon taking the rain and thunder chances with it.\nUp to 1/4\" of rain is expected south of I96 with 1/4\"-1/2\" expected \nnorth.\n \nDry air behind the front along with high pressure will keep fair \nconditions in place later Wednesday through Friday.\n\n- Low chance of rain Saturday\n\nOur next chance of precipitation, albeit a low one, arrives Saturday \nas a weak shortwave drives a front through. Between the best \ndynamics being displaced from the area, and little in the way of \nmoisture return ahead of the front, PoPs are low with little \nprecipitation favored to occur.\n\n- Additional showers and storms Monday\n\nA stronger system takes aim at the region Monday as the upper-level \njet moves south into the region. A deepening low associated with \nthis will lead to potentially breezy conditions as well as the \nchance for showers and storms. NBM probabilities suggest a 30-40% \nchance of over 1\" of rainfall, highest across the northwest CWA, so \nwill need to monitor for potential heavy rainfall.",
